Module pellam "The Liberty of the Ice" {
  Description "What lies under the Fair and off its critical path: the cut leaf in the Book of Frosts, the left glove, the egg that crosses the bridge each Lady Day, the boys on sheep-bone skates, a fish and a flame in the Saint's scales, the Collegium's brass ell on the Exchange floor; and the Fair's two foreshadowings, which pay off in the morning's news.".

  // ── The count and its first page ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The count is the Book of Frosts, kept by Gaffer Tredgold for the Company and by the Company for the river. What it is
  // a count of is not frosts. It was begun to write down what the river did with what was shaken on it. The first leaf
  // has been cut out.
  Discovery book_stub "The Book of Frosts has a stub where its first leaf was, cut out cleanly with a knife: eight leaves went into the gathering and seven frosts are in it. Tredgold's father said the leaf was cut by the first Reeve, Aldith, before she had written a word on it.".
  Topic tredgold_stub with tredgold "Ask Gaffer Tredgold about the stub at the front of the Book of Frosts" when knows(actor, book_of_frosts) once {
    Says "'That.' He opens the Book from the back, as the Company reads it, and lays a thick finger on a finger's breadth of torn edge ahead of the First Frost. 'Eight leaves went into the making of this gathering and there are seven frosts in it. Count it yourself.' He does not take his finger away. 'My father said Reeve Aldith cut it with her own knife before she had put a word on it. Why would you cut a leaf before you wrote on it?' He shuts the Book. 'I have a notion. I keep it to myself. I told my own boy: write what happened, not what you think.' He looks at the saws by the door. 'It is not a wolf leaf, if that is what you are asking. Four of the seven have wolves in them. That one is blank.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, book_stub). }
  }

  // ── The Glove ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Discovery left_glove "The Glove on the Fair's mast is always a left glove, cut by the Glovers' Company from a pattern in the Company's chest and sewn a size too small for any man; the Reeve shakes with her right.".
  Topic tredgold_glove with tredgold "Ask Gaffer Tredgold why the Glove on the mast is always a left glove" when true once {
    Says "'Is it?' He looks pleased, as a man does who is asked something nobody has asked in thirty years. 'It is. The Glovers' Company has the pattern in a chest at the back of their hall, and it comes out in a frost and goes back after. They sew it a size small, so no man's hand will go in it. A man who can get his hand in the Glove, they say, has got above himself.' He takes up his pen. 'The Reeve shakes with her right; that is the Court's. The left is the Glove's. Ask me why and I will tell you what I told the last man who asked, which was a canon from Veyra with a notebook: it was a left glove before there was a Company.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, left_glove). }
  }

  // ── An egg on a spoon ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Discovery bridge_egg "Every Lady Day a Bankside child carries an egg on a spoon from the Bankside end of Pellam Bridge to its midpoint, while an alderman of Pellam stands there and watches; if it is dropped Bankside owes the city a bell. It has not been dropped in living memory.".
  Topic nab_egg with nab "Ask Nab Pollard why a Bankside child carries an egg on a spoon to the middle of the bridge every Lady Day" when true once {
    Says "'Toll.' He is polishing a cup with the rag he polishes everything with. 'After the Frost They Hanged the Reeve, Bankside said she had done right, and Pellam said she had not, and Bankside stood her stool at the middle of the bridge for a year with a candle on it. When the city took it down they came to terms. We could keep our opinion if we paid for it. An egg. On a spoon. From our end to the middle, with an alderman looking on, and if it drops, a bell.' He holds the cup to the light. 'It has not dropped in my time, nor my father's. Last Lady Day it was Hester Pole's lad, nine years old, and he was as white as the egg.'".
    On discuss { Assert knows(actor, bridge_egg). }
  }

  // ── Sheep-bone skates, which a particular morning will account for ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Seen by everyone on the first day. It is the foreshadowing that always pays: the morning's news returns to them.
  Observe bone_skates at freezeland when firstDay() and not iceGone() and not dark() "Under the Glove Post the boys of Bankside are skating on the shin-bones of sheep, strapped to their boots with thongs, standing on one leg to push with the other; and the ice sings under them in one long scraped note, like a hundred slow knives on a stone. A very small one has lost a bone already and is going round and round on his own two feet, shrieking, with the other boot off.".
  Observe bone_skates_late at freezeland when thirdDay() and not iceGone() and not dark() "The boys are not skating today. They sit along the edge of the Fair with the sheep-bones in their laps, looking at the ice, which has gone the grey of old pewter, and which has stopped singing.".

  // ── A fish and over it a flame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Found in at most five adventures, explained in none. Here it is in the pivot of the Saint's scales. The verger will
  // not say whose it is.
  Discovery scale_mark "Cut small into the brass of the pivot of Saint Evenhand's great scales, under the polish of the hands: a fish, and over it a flame. The brass of the scales has been cast round it." {
    Found by searching evenhands with insight "You go down on one knee by the pillar with the Saint's beam above you and look where a hand would go. In the pivot, under the polish of a great many Sundays, a fish and over it a flame, very small, cut before the brass was cast.".
  }
  Topic verger_mark with verger "Ask the verger about the little fish and flame in the pivot of the scales" when knows(actor, scale_mark) once {
    Says "'Ah.' He puts a thumb over it, as a man covers a child's ears. 'It is called the Saint's mark. It is not. The Saint's mark is a pair of scales; I looked, in the saints' book, when I was young and had nothing to do.' He takes the thumb away. 'My master said you do not scour it, you do not gild it, you do not ask whose it is.'".
  }
  Topic verger_measures with verger "Ask the verger whose stamp is on the factors' weights" when knows(actor, scales_known) once {
    Says "'Sennet's.' He says it with the weary relish of an old man correcting a young one. 'The Collegium of Measures. They weigh, mind: they do not teach. That is Veyra. Sennet weighs, and stamps what it weighs, and every weight in Fleece Row has the Collegium's mark on its under side.' He nods at the great brass pan. 'The Saint's weights have nobody's. I asked once. Sennet wrote back that they had no standard to stamp them against.'".
  }

  // ── The Exchange floor: a brass ell, and a board nobody has answered ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  Observe ell_scene at exchange_floor when true "Let into the flags of the Exchange floor, a bar of brass the length of a long stride with a row of nail-holes along its edge, and at one end the small round stamp of the Collegium of Measures at Sennet: THE ELL OF PELLAM, SEALED. Factors lay their cloth on it with a flourish and their thumbs on the line. The seal is renewed every seventh year by a Fellow who comes up the North Road with a case and goes home the same week; the last one came before the snow and did not like the sky.".
  Observe exchange_board at exchange_floor when not iceGone() "By the door, chalked in a clerk's round hand and not rubbed out since Michaelmas: FOUNDERS' EXCHANGE: FLEECE BY THE HEAD, SPRING DELIVERY. ASK FOR THE FACTOR'S CARTER. Nobody has asked for the carter in a month. The roads are shut.".

  // ── The Calf, and a stranger's story ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // What a walker brought in ahead of the snow, and what Pellam makes of it. Read only if the Slack has been opened.
  Observe sennet_rumour at calf_taproom when befell(slack_opened) and not iceGone() "A drover who walked in on the last road before the snow is telling three watermen and a dog that at Sennet, on the coast, in the great House of Doors, the floor of the hall has been lifted, and a stair goes down under it a great deal further than the cellar could. He is the only man in the room who is not lying, and nobody believes him.".

  // ── The morning's news: what the Fair's two small things come to ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Rule news_skate when newsHour() and not officeRead(lisk) and not officeSung(lisk) once says "Of the bone skates: the watermen collected them from the edge of the Fair at dusk, forty-one pairs in a barrow, and when the ice had gone and the water dropped a little at the foot of Swan Stairs there was one more in the weed, a very small one, brown as a nut and a great deal older than any of the others. Nobody claimed it. It lay on the top step all night, and this morning it is not there." { }
  Rule news_skate_office when newsHour() and officeRead(lisk) and not officeSung(lisk) once says "Of the bone skates: the watermen collected them from the edge of the Fair at dusk, forty-one pairs in a barrow, and when the ice had gone and the water dropped a little at the foot of Swan Stairs there was one more in the weed, a very small one, brown as a nut and a great deal older than any of the others. Widow Saffery was on the top step before it was light, and she took it up as one takes up a sleeping child, and has hung it on the lantern-hook in her window, where the bridge can see it." { }
  Rule news_skate_song when newsHour() and officeSung(lisk) once says "Of the bone skates: the watermen collected them from the edge of the Fair at dusk, forty-one pairs in a barrow, and when the ice had gone and the water dropped a little at the foot of Swan Stairs there was one more in the weed, a very small one, brown as a nut. Widow Saffery was on the top step before it was light. She took it up and carried it indoors, and the lantern-hook in her window is empty." { }
  Rule news_candle when newsHour() and knows(party, candle_office) and hiredLanded(lisk) and not hiredFled(lisk) once says "Of the churchwarden: Master Harker was in the Scale Church at first light, before the verger, and the Saint's candle burned that morning in the thaw, which is not the season; and he sat under the board in gold with his hat on his knee, which is not like him. The verger says the beam did not move." { }
  Rule news_candle_fled when newsHour() and knows(party, candle_office) and (hiredFled(lisk) or hiredReleased(lisk)) once says "Of the churchwarden: Master Harker sent to the chandler on Swan Lane at first light for a second pound of wax, in his own hand, and had the verger set it beside the first. Asked for whom, he said, for the weight. The verger asked him which." { }
}
